Temporomandibular Joint (TMJ) Dysfunction
Opthalmology & ENT
TMJ localized pain, Jaw clicking/crepitus, Restricted mouth opening

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-043
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe facial pain, locked jaw, and inability to chew or speak properly.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with acute, severe, unilateral facial pain and a functionally locked jaw (restricted opening). Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out mandibular dislocation, acute temporal arteritis, or a severe dental abscess, and to provide immediate pharmacological and mechanical interventions to release the severe muscular spasm preventing normal oral intake and speech prior to routine hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My jaw clicked loudly during dinner, then locked up entirely. The pain is shooting straight into my ear and temple."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a dull, aching, unilateral facial pain directly localized precisely over the left temporomandibular joint, which acutely worsened 2 hours ago while chewing hard food. Describes the jaw unexpectedly locking in a firmly closed position, with severely restricted maximum interincisal opening. Endorses distinct mechanical clicking and popping consistently felt during mandibular motion prior to the locking. Pain radiates continuously to the ear and temporal region. Clear history of severe daily psychological stress, chronic tension-type headaches, and nocturnal bruxism. Denies fever, jaw trauma, or visual changes.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, speaking with clenched teeth, unable to eat.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high stress levels contributing to pathology.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, massaging the left side of the face, visibly anxious.
Physical Exam: Pronounced, exquisite tenderness to manual palpation of the muscles of mastication (left masseter and temporalis). Localized tenderness directly over the left TMJ space. Maximum interincisal opening is restricted to 15mm (trismus secondary to muscle spasm). Upon guided, gentle opening, a distinct mechanical click is palpated at the left TMJ. The mandible is NOT dislocated (condyle is palpable within the fossa, not anterior to the articular eminence). Bilateral tympanic membranes are perfectly normal (ruling out primary otitis). No temporal artery tenderness.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s desk is covered in work documents; signs of high stress.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Temporomandibular Joint (TMJ) Dysfunction with Acute Muscle Spasm - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Anterior Mandibular Dislocation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The jaw being locked closed (trismus) rather than locked wide open, and the condyle being palpated within the glenoid fossa.

Giant Cell Arteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient's younger demographic, normal temporal artery palpation, and the pain being distinctly mechanical, triggered by chewing and localized to the masticatory muscles, rather than a systemic vasculitis.

Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pristine, normal tympanic membranes on otoscopy; the ear pain is entirely referred from the adjacent TMJ.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with severe facial pain and a functionally locked jaw, mimicking a joint dislocation or severe localized infection. Urgent clinical examination was necessary to confirm the condyle was structurally seated within the joint and to exclude primary otologic or vascular emergencies. By confirming profound masseter/temporalis spasm and TMJ localized pain without dislocation, the visit prevented an unnecessary emergency department presentation for suspected dislocation or stroke, and facilitated immediate conservative management using heat, NSAIDs, and muscle relaxant techniques to safely restore jaw function in the home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Ibuprofen 400mg and Paracetamol 1g STAT. Prescribed a short 3-day course of oral Diazepam 2mg at night strictly for severe acute masseter spasm release. Advised an absolute soft-food diet for 7 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: To break the unconscious habit of daytime bruxism and clenching, the patient must place brightly colored sticky notes on their computer monitor and bathroom mirror. Every time they see a note, they must actively drop their jaw, separate their teeth, and rest their tongue gently on the roof of their mouth. The immediate physical relaxation of the masseter muscle breaks the pain cycle (positive reinforcement), structurally modifying the stress-induced clenching behavior.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days; highly recommend a dental referral for a nocturnal occlusal splint (bite guard) if bruxism continues.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the jaw suddenly locks in a wide-open position and cannot be closed, if severe swelling develops under the chin or in the neck, or if a high fever occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a functional musculoskeletal issue; chronic management requires dental/maxillofacial input.
Clinical Course: The acute locking and severe pain will ease over 48 hours with anti-inflammatories, heat, and rest. The clicking may persist long-term.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your jaw locks completely wide open and you cannot physically close it, it has dislocated and requires emergency hospital reduction.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Stress and nighttime grinding have severely overworked your jaw muscles, causing them to go into a painful, locked spasm. The joint itself is inflamed, causing the clicking. The muscle relaxants and anti-inflammatories will break the spasm.
Actionable Steps: Apply a warm, moist towel to the side of your face for 15 minutes, four times a day. Eat only soft foods (soup, yogurt, mashed potatoes) for the next week. Do not chew gum or attempt wide yawns.

